steps.body:
The filled table holds lengths, but it also shows how each length was made, so you can read one longest subsequence back out of it. Start at the bottom right corner, `dp[n][m]`, and walk toward row 0 or column 0:

1. If `a[i - 1] == b[j - 1]`, that character is in the answer. Keep it and step diagonally, up and left.
2. Otherwise step toward the bigger neighbor: up drops the last character of `a`, left drops the last of `b`.
3. Stop when `i` or `j` reaches 0, then reverse what you kept, since it comes out last first.

With `dp` filled for `a = "stone"` and `b = "longest"`:

```cpp harness
std::string a = "stone";
std::string b = "longest";
int n = static_cast<int>(a.size());
int m = static_cast<int>(b.size());
std::vector<std::vector<int>> dp(n + 1, std::vector<int>(m + 1, 0));
for (int i = 1; i <= n; ++i) {
    for (int j = 1; j <= m; ++j) {
        if (a[i - 1] == b[j - 1]) {
            dp[i][j] = dp[i - 1][j - 1] + 1;
        } else {
            dp[i][j] = std::max(dp[i - 1][j], dp[i][j - 1]);
        }
    }
}
{{code}}
```

```cpp
int i = n;
int j = m;
std::string out;
while (i > 0 && j > 0) {
    if (a[i - 1] == b[j - 1]) {
        out += a[i - 1];
        --i;
        --j;
    } else if (dp[i - 1][j] >= dp[i][j - 1]) {
        --i;   // a tie steps up
    } else {
        --j;
    }
}
std::reverse(out.begin(), out.end());
std::cout << out << '\n';   // one
```

When the cell above equals the cell to the left, both ways lead to a longest answer, but often to different ones. `ab` and `ba` share `a`, and also `b`. So fix a tie rule and keep it: here `>=` steps up on a tie, which gives `a`, while stepping left would give `b`.

Each step lowers `i`, `j`, or both, so the walk takes at most n + m steps after the O(n * m) fill.

