steps.body:
Edit distance is the fewest edits that turn `a` into `b`, where one edit inserts, deletes, or replaces a single character. `dp[i][j]` is the distance from the first `i` characters of `a` to the first `j` of `b`.

This time the empty prefixes aren't 0. Turning `i` characters into nothing takes `i` deletes, and turning nothing into `j` characters takes `j` inserts. So column 0 counts from 0 to n, and row 0 from 0 to m.

For every other cell, compare the last characters. Equal ones need no edit, so the cell copies `dp[i - 1][j - 1]`. Otherwise the last edit was one of three, so take 1 plus the smallest:

- `dp[i - 1][j]`: delete the last character of `a`.
- `dp[i][j - 1]`: insert the last character of `b`.
- `dp[i - 1][j - 1]`: replace one with the other.

```cpp
std::string a = "kitten";
std::string b = "sitting";
int n = static_cast<int>(a.size());
int m = static_cast<int>(b.size());
std::vector<std::vector<int>> dp(n + 1, std::vector<int>(m + 1, 0));
for (int i = 0; i <= n; ++i) {
    dp[i][0] = i;
}
for (int j = 0; j <= m; ++j) {
    dp[0][j] = j;
}
for (int i = 1; i <= n; ++i) {
    for (int j = 1; j <= m; ++j) {
        if (a[i - 1] == b[j - 1]) {
            dp[i][j] = dp[i - 1][j - 1];
        } else {
            dp[i][j] = 1 + std::min({dp[i - 1][j], dp[i][j - 1], dp[i - 1][j - 1]});
        }
    }
}
std::cout << dp[n][m] << '\n';   // 3
```

`std::min` with braces takes a whole list, so it compares all three at once. The 3 edits are: replace `k` with `s`, replace `e` with `i`, and insert `g`. It's the same table as the LCS, so it's O(n * m) time and space too.

*That ends the data structures and algorithms. Next, the advanced track starts with **Initialization: Braces, Narrowing, and the Most Vexing Parse**, and climbs from there to the C++ that low-latency and quant code is built on.*

